<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Shu Han</h1><p class="st-meta">221 CE – 263 CE · capital Chengdu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Three Kingdoms state of Liu Bei and Zhuge Liang in Sichuan, which claimed to continue the Han dynasty and became the heroic centre of the Romance of the Three Kingdoms.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, a distant Han kinsman, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan">Sun Quan</a> to defeat <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-red-cliffs">Red Cliffs</a> in 208, then took Yi province (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>) in 214 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a> in 219. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-pi">Cao Pi</a> ended the Han in 220, Liu Bei proclaimed himself emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> in 221, presenting his state as the legitimate Han.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei's</a> attack on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eastern-wu">Eastern Wu</a> ended in disaster at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-xiaoting">Yiling</a> in 222, and he died in 223. The chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> governed for his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a>, with strict law, irrigation works and salt and iron monopolies. He pacified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanzhong">Nanzhong</a> south in 225 and led five northern expeditions against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a>, dying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-wuzhang-plains">Wuzhang Plains</a> in 234.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-wei">Jiang Wei</a> continued the northern campaigns, exhausting the state. In 263 the Wei general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-ai">Deng Ai</a> crossed the mountains to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-shan">Liu Shan</a> surrendered. The later novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-yu">Guan Yu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-fei">Zhang Fei</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuge-liang">Zhuge Liang</a> lasting heroes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/shu-han.jpg" alt="Shu Han" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Three_Kingdoms.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>221 CE – 263 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chengdu</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Liu Bei, Liu Shan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Shu, Han (Three Kingdoms)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=242&amp;focus=shu-han" aria-label="Shu Han on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7376 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Shu Han lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7696 1315L7769 1191M7696 1315L7855 1179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7769 1191h0M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7696 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7696 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chengdu. <a href="/atlas?year=242&amp;focus=shu-han">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
